              <l xml:id="m-00287a81-5ab8-4b63-aa05-f3360794d3d2" facs="#m-928c2787-a524-4bb1-8697-7ed1c91a9588" label="text-like">Rules for Interpretation.</l><l xml:id="m-c6245b69-0caf-4ad7-a6cb-0b00247d1bde" facs="#m-b9e809ac-8e00-4531-9092-334ba8116020" label="ambiguous">xxxIij.</l><l xml:id="m-94fdd26e-4a52-4ec5-a24f-09a768075f3b" facs="#m-d718466a-df49-4904-bc09-a43c93b9cc4c" label="text-like">especially in the more important words. There must be no cut or break</l><l xml:id="m-3527d396-d909-4869-9c84-4d30fb91f91d" facs="#m-afddff35-15a0-49ab-9aec-903d053bd7fb" label="text-like">interrupting the regular flow of the recitation from the beginning to the Mediation</l><l xml:id="m-9ff93309-8d19-4470-980c-26ccc6dcf0bb" facs="#m-67c8fa88-f1f3-4798-ba7f-0630acb8ab2e" label="text-like">and thence to the Final Cadence. There must be life and movement but no</l><l xml:id="m-8d435af8-59af-4a8a-a892-2cbb16687689" facs="#m-ba432b1d-23aa-4088-8bcc-07b8aed9bc7f" label="text-like">hurry; the singing is the"Opus Dei" —God's work.</l><l xml:id="m-0ff746e8-b793-4a1e-b0c5-cb1bd2d38b6d" facs="#m-6291f8b0-74bb-4514-8a92-deebbde493cd" label="text-like">Cadences. In this book the Cadences of each Tone or mode are set out at the</l><l xml:id="m-f6d4a1f4-66b2-41d5-b65e-82b2d698a1bf" facs="#m-1f4fada0-90a6-4715-8be7-f74a04077ed0" label="text-like">beginning of the Psalm. The choice of the Final Cadence, where there are</l><l xml:id="m-5e4d1320-6551-4b32-b3de-5a7a393e7be7" facs="#m-2ee4db3a-ee4a-4fa1-9eab-3ac434bdaea4" label="text-like">several, is determined by the Antiphon.</l><l xml:id="m-9add3524-92b3-4d4a-9413-b99c2ff48d52" facs="#m-bdd040e3-a93a-43c3-8765-3ad9fbc78e77" label="text-like">Cadences are of two kinds:</l><l xml:id="m-1f12eb0d-b408-4e43-b52c-56951b30a655" facs="#m-9b8dc0da-4e7a-4f29-9946-19ee18244024" label="lyrics-like">spondaic.)Dómi-no mé¬</l><l xml:id="m-85354a18-f23e-45bf-9864-5d9dcc14c501" facs="#m-6a3e091f-aa4a-4401-8eb3-7f045a69ba33" label="text-like">A. Cadences of one accent. eg</l><l xml:id="m-0aa45e80-abb9-48c3-90b2-41d4de4cfa7b" facs="#m-75618878-ca44-4691-8e37-c0a70b940b73" label="ambiguous">o</l><l xml:id="m-22aba3e8-2629-4ab0-a5ec-911c608ea7ef" facs="#m-55fb9db1-6067-423b-a4d3-0d35884e00ae" label="lyrics-like">ipû-e-ri Dómi-num</l><l xml:id="m-f3f0f930-82d2-4647-a481-69617fefe0b4" facs="#m-fe4ecc45-701e-46e0-8377-f739575695ce" label="lyrics-like">in tó-to cór- de mé¬</l><l xml:id="m-58f8c747-b4fc-4cce-b3f0-4b094859ad2b" facs="#m-e0d98636-07d4-43c1-9198-48f141518ae5" label="ambiguous">o</l><l xml:id="m-6f54a353-8039-47ae-b878-40900f93f95e" facs="#m-956681d0-494c-4736-be48-44fcbc18df6a" label="lyrics-like">pú-e- ri Dómi-num</l><l xml:id="m-3db7076b-7068-46c4-b2ac-eb45d6f254a1" facs="#m-b3371c2f-170e-49e9-8a7c-1c2cc2784ab6" label="text-like">B. Cadences of two accents. e g.</l><l xml:id="m-9e6244b0-c966-48ad-acce-197a2129d18c" facs="#m-b80f8fbb-425c-4836-8c92-f3248f30eee8" label="lyrics-like">fé¬</l><l xml:id="m-18a6a0fb-1ced-42a6-adbd-c1ca5bba9fa0" facs="#m-aac3c19c-c8b8-4494-92e8-878611c71de5" label="lyrics-like">có-ram te</l><l xml:id="m-ea6398d9-acd4-4312-a196-c21824821f28" facs="#m-63471ca6-a24d-42fe-be11-607365e8defc" label="ambiguous">ci</l><l xml:id="m-8dd676ae-59d6-4917-9eb8-469a52dcbc30" facs="#m-8026527f-8c75-40e7-b616-55cb777eb3a7" label="ambiguous">co- gno¬ vi-sti me</l><l xml:id="m-19c5b34a-4e9d-4aed-bfde-0a209bd6db3c" facs="#m-74b00edf-2ccc-4938-8a50-188921a3afb9" label="text-like">It will be noticed that when a word accented on the third last syllable occurs,</l><l xml:id="m-dfbc259f-44a1-4312-99dc-9a960cc89605" facs="#m-96821a54-4781-43ed-9f1e-65bbba4e0447" label="text-like">an extra note, (printed hollow thus o), has to be used in the Cadence. Moreover,</l><l xml:id="m-dcaa03f2-bfd7-421a-81c7-ee003543ff16" facs="#m-37a1b090-70c1-4bf3-bea6-8e7a93348468" label="text-like">a great many Cadences have one, two or three syllables preparatory to the</l><l xml:id="m-bf533fb4-1d5b-42ee-83aa-d82f678a1190" facs="#m-54f9d7cc-0809-46af-b042-0f879569c78e" label="text-like">accent. In the Psalms of the Office these Cadences are easily recognised because</l><l xml:id="m-c82c78b7-6e73-47e9-8c49-ab6bbc1f4ff8" facs="#m-86d75f1e-f670-4a3b-b647-f7201f3a9811" label="text-like">they leave the Reciting note in a descending movement. (a)</l><l xml:id="m-8d845e28-23ea-4f45-973b-963718bec154" facs="#m-661bafbf-e8a1-494a-9b20-d7f88c4c8aef" label="ambiguous">Modes</l><l xml:id="m-96452d4e-12d9-49f0-baf3-6bc7219f1c31" facs="#m-a83aaa03-5e2a-4602-83a5-84f650c9b53e" label="ambiguous" /><l xml:id="m-b13c50b3-0766-427a-9377-f91192620957" facs="#m-6f57436f-f942-4ff1-b505-c202b69b7e11" label="lyrics-like">Dómi-nus Dó- mi-[no mé¬</l><l xml:id="m-d588e690-ed54-461f-afcd-d627c502b309" facs="#m-446e24a6-e65e-4a50-8b69-3c9fa8a5f29e" label="ambiguous">o</l><l xml:id="m-9c79ea27-16ff-4610-8e56-b17107572698" facs="#m-015097e4-796c-4bb1-aedf-5553c05375b3" label="text-like">The passage from the</l><l xml:id="m-01d08659-d1b5-4b0c-bb14-9967b538c055" facs="#m-e218a58e-ca8f-4d84-939b-ae99497c4343" label="text-like">Reciting Note to the</l><l xml:id="m-43e4b68f-3790-4735-888d-b7fffb5f151c" facs="#m-a6d195af-29aa-4634-9664-896a00957dc7" label="ambiguous">2</l><l xml:id="m-45cb0f91-fe24-4d96-8e47-7c7df2291e73" facs="#m-afbaf80b-b2ce-4d57-978c-e40861dda5f1" label="text-like">accent is made by one</l><l xml:id="m-dd61e451-4bcd-4a7c-a22d-f58da5f69625" facs="#m-a435a424-df36-4545-9191-4c5bb6d56705" label="text-like">two or three prepara¬</l><l xml:id="m-6e27c934-e375-44f9-9858-06664f76455e" facs="#m-5d47e145-7d16-4134-b0a1-5ff383cd44d8" label="lyrics-like">Dómi-nus Dó-mi- no mé¬</l><l xml:id="m-c6ebf6dd-41fa-4897-ac73-66332c23d1a8" facs="#m-4d127a24-4e21-4c1d-94b6-af50d6f68b97" label="ambiguous">tory syllables. e. g.</l><l xml:id="m-dd49fb4d-6dd4-4b0e-b56b-f30ad0a2a2e2" facs="#m-d059d5d7-40c3-4871-a197-6f92351e5631" label="ambiguous">o</l><l xml:id="m-2e70cb7b-f932-4de1-9486-48d352c2a5b4" facs="#m-b9458d97-8d13-42da-abe3-adfe5b2964cb" label="ambiguous">3.</l><l xml:id="m-9ecb3834-9f7c-4a33-87b9-f41dca909827" facs="#m-1e64b611-fa27-4234-9c4d-c2a55419392e" label="ambiguous">Pereg.</l><l xml:id="m-6bcfef4f-e9d9-4b29-8ae3-e20b6bd338be" facs="#m-8fbda566-e60e-44dd-8895-b668bd4b1fcd" label="lyrics-like">Adji-ci-at lDó-mi-nus sú-per vos</l><l xml:id="m-52436ad7-2aee-45ee-8f47-19b9ac08a8ff" facs="#m-7fe636b8-9464-4851-a041-88a8a9485c5e" label="text-like">What has hitherto been said suggests four questions of practical interest; we</l><l xml:id="m-311e3817-06d1-4966-9677-d539e5b311ac" facs="#m-9e45acc5-b25f-431b-91d7-71e5617ecbcd" label="text-like">mention them here with the solutions given with the Psalms for Vesper¬</l><l xml:id="m-799b7854-6480-42b9-8cfc-d76b25c6a2de" facs="#m-1905305a-b01e-460a-a564-117c26aceccd" label="ambiguous">(p. 128. et seq).</l><l xml:id="m-33375bed-0e0f-4215-a603-2decf22292af" facs="#m-424f3ad4-bca4-4907-89f1-01977c63adc4" label="text-like">1. How determine the choice of Cadences suitable to the words?</l><l xml:id="m-01e02cec-9065-4589-ac5d-9873e8ec7ecf" facs="#m-a613f415-561f-4eca-b260-a317c253c1f8" label="text-like">Answer: by looking at the Rubric at the beginning of each Psalm.</l><l xml:id="m-5355eb42-93c9-4002-8c55-cbad03483b9b" facs="#m-4bdd88c7-c28b-4cc6-af27-783dfafc3585" label="text-like">(a) Two exceptions may be mentioned : the Mediation Cadence of the Solemn sth Mode, and</l><l xml:id="m-473c26e9-c252-443b-9994-6a8b12bbb17e" facs="#m-ea411eec-fe77-44f8-9e07-6ec2d9264622" label="text-like">that of the“Tonus in directum" used at Compline on Holy Saturdav.</l><l xml:id="m-6c871df1-889b-408f-b577-dc9bddd39944" facs="#m-6f98c58f-a1ef-4b82-a1bd-01e6cf6b1663" label="ambiguous">N° S01. — 2</l></lg></div></section></score></mdiv></body></music>
</mei>